Name : Port Security Shutdown Mode

Why we did this
================
Protect Mode silently drops unauthorized traffic.
Restrict Mode drops unauthorized traffic and generates logs.
However, in high-security environments such as banks, data centers, government networks, and critical infrastructure, simply dropping the traffic is not enough.
Administrators want immediate action whenever an unauthorized device is connected.
To provide the highest level of Layer 2 port security, Cisco introduced Shutdown Mode.
Shutdown Mode completely disables the port whenever a security violation occurs.

What will happen
================
1. The switch learns the allowed MAC address(es).
2. Authorized devices communicate normally.
3. If an unauthorized device connects:
   - Security violation occurs.
   - Port immediately enters Err-Disabled state.
   - All traffic stops.
   - Syslog messages are generated.
   - SNMP traps can be generated.
   - Administrator intervention is usually required.

Example:

Allowed MAC:
0011.2233.4455

Unauthorized MAC:
00AA.BBCC.DDEE

Result:
- Port is shut down.
- All communication stops.
- User loses connectivity.
- Network team investigates the incident.

Advantages and Disadvantages of this, hence what came next
==========================================================
Advantages
----------
1. Highest level of Port Security protection.
2. Immediately blocks unauthorized devices.
3. Generates security logs and alerts.
4. Helps detect physical security violations.
5. Prevents repeated attack attempts on the same port.
6. Widely used in secure enterprise environments.

Disadvantages
-------------
1. Legitimate users may lose connectivity if their device is replaced.
2. Requires manual recovery or automatic err-disable recovery.
3. Can generate support tickets for simple user mistakes.
4. Operational overhead is higher than Protect and Restrict modes.
5. Not ideal for frequently changing environments.

Hence what came next
--------------------
Administrators noticed that manually configuring MAC addresses was still difficult.
They wanted the switch to automatically learn and save MAC addresses while still enforcing Port Security.
This led to:
Static MAC Binding
and later
Sticky MAC Security
which automatically learns and stores MAC addresses to reduce administrative effort.

One-Shot Configuration
======================
Switch(config)# interface fa0/1
Switch(config-if)# switchport mode access
Switch(config-if)# switchport port-security
Switch(config-if)# switchport port-security maximum 1
Switch(config-if)# switchport port-security mac-address 0011.2233.4455
Switch(config-if)# switchport port-security violation shutdown
Switch(config-if)# end

Verification
===========
show port-security
show port-security interface fa0/1

Expected Result
===============
authorized Device:
------------------
Traffic Allowed

Unauthorized Device:
--------------------
Port Shutdown

Port Status:
-------------
Err-Disabled

Logs:
-----
Generated

SNMP Traps:
-----------
Generated (if configured)

Recovery Commands
=================

Switch(config)# interface fa0/1
Switch(config-if)# shutdown
Switch(config-if)# no shutdown

OR

Switch(config)# errdisable recovery cause psecure-violation
Switch(config)# errdisable recovery interval 300

Summary
=======

Problem:
Protect and Restrict Modes allowed the port to stay operational after a violation.

Solution:
Shutdown Mode immediately disables the port when an unauthorized device is detected.

Benefit:
Provides the strongest security among all Port Security violation modes.

Limitation:
Can cause operational disruption and requires recovery actions.

